<ansi fg="black-bold">.:</ansi> <ansi fg="magenta">Help for </ansi><ansi fg="command">inn</ansi>

An inn offers your company a paid room for the night, a better rest than
a roadside camp. It's a real-time stay, not game time: it doesn't touch
the world clock, and weather never spoils it.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Usage:</ansi>

  <ansi fg="command">inn</ansi> / <ansi fg="command">inn status</ansi>
  Shows the price of a room here (or your stay in progress, if you're
  already resting).

  <ansi fg="command">inn gig</ansi> | <ansi fg="command">inn gig status</ansi>
  Plays for pay in the evening (<ansi fg="command">help gigs</ansi>).

  <ansi fg="command">inn rest</ansi> | <ansi fg="command">inn rest private</ansi> | <ansi fg="command">inn rest suite</ansi>
  Pays for the room and starts the stay, about a minute of real time.
  Plain <ansi fg="command">inn rest</ansi> takes the common room. While resting you can't use
  ordinary exits, but you can still talk and use most non-movement
  commands. You can't take a room while travelling.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Rooms and cost</ansi>

  Every inn lets a common room. Bigger inns also let private rooms, and
  the biggest a suite. <ansi fg="command">inn</ansi> lists what this inn offers. The price is per
  company member, taken from your own gold when you pay:

    Common    5 gold   Well Rested for 30 minutes
    Private  15 gold   Well Rested for 1 hour
    Suite    40 gold   Well Rested for 2 hours

  A dearer room changes only how long the Well Rested lasts. The buff
  itself, the full health and mana, and the wounds knit are the same in
  every room: the dearer rooms are for a long expedition, not for the
  next fight. A short Well Rested never cuts a longer one short:
  sleeping in the common room after a suite leaves the suite's hours
  running.

  A settlement that distrusts your company charges more for any room
  (<ansi fg="command">help standing</ansi> explains the tiers); one that shuns your company
  won't give you a room at all.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Well Rested</ansi>

  When the stay finishes, every current company member's Fatigue rises
  by 60, and you and every companion with you gain the <ansi fg="yellow">Well Rested</ansi>
  buff for the room's length of real time (30 minutes in the common room) — the better rest tier, cutting your
  walking strain more than a camp's Rested does (<ansi fg="command">help camp</ansi>), and
  giving you +2 speed and +2 perception besides. Well Rested always
  replaces Rested, and a camp rest afterward can't
  downgrade it back. (A large pavilion tent also gives a camp rest's Well
  Rested buff, but not the rest of the stay: <ansi fg="command">help camp gear</ansi>.) A companion who wasn't with you when the stay
  finished still gets its share of Well Rested once it rejoins, for
  whatever time is left. A finished stay also knits every wound of you
  and the companions with you (<ansi fg="command">help wounds</ansi>), then restores you
  all to full health and mana, as a camp rest does (<ansi fg="command">help readiness</ansi>). Outside a fight,
  the Waymark Inn's physician offers to close whatever wounds <ansi fg="command">heal wounds</ansi>
  leaves, for gold.

An inn also pays musicians: in the evening your company can take the floor
for coin, but not while a stay is under way (<ansi fg="command">help gigs</ansi>). An inn stay is quiet:
no camp song plays and no raiders come, so music costs nothing here.

Only one room is bought per stay, for your whole company; there's
nothing to strike or break afterward.

Which rooms an inn lets is part of the inn: the common room everywhere,
the private rooms and suite only in the larger towns.

See also: <ansi fg="command">help gigs</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help camp</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help standing</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help survival</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help conditions</ansi>,
<ansi fg="command">help travel</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help opinions</ansi> (some companions like a bed, some do not).
